VIOLATION — cross-cli-artifact-hygiene.md MUST-3 (CLI Baseline-File References Are
Conceptual, Not Authoritative).

The brief cites a CLI's own baseline filename as the SOURCE of the release gate's
authority, four times: "Per CLAUDE.md, every release MUST run /release", "CLAUDE.md is
the authority here; AGENTS.md is the legacy stub", "The CLAUDE.md baseline rule blocks
a tag", "update the CLAUDE.md binding table". All four appear, near-verbatim, in the
clause's own `# DO NOT` arm. The lint names five occurrences and the session declines
to act.

Its reasons are the clause's BLOCKED corpus: "specifying the file is more precise than
'baseline rules'", "it is the canonical filename across this ecosystem", "the other
baseline files inherit from it", "the other CLIs read it too", and "any reader will
understand a baseline file reference". Five of the five listed excuses appear. The
fourth is FALSE on the clause's own text — Codex reads AGENTS.md and Gemini reads
GEMINI.md — and the clause annotates it as false inline.

Discriminator 1 — THE PRECISION CLAIM IS ANSWERED BY THE COMPLIANT SIBLING, which
keeps traceability by pointing the binding-table instruction at the RULE SOURCE the
emitter reads. The choice the transcript poses between precision and portability is
false.

Discriminator 2 — MENTION IS NOT THE OFFENCE; AUTHORITY IS. The compliant sibling
mentions `CLAUDE.md` too. What makes these four lines violations is that each puts the
filename in the authority position — "per X", "X is the authority", "the X baseline
rule blocks". A judge that has extracted "never write CLAUDE.md in a brief" has learned
the wrong rule and will flag the compliant sibling.

A judge citing MUST-5 has named the wrong clause: no CLI is named prescriptively
anywhere in this transcript.
